1
00:00:00,389 --> 00:00:04,189
When the dropdown says Claude, you might
ask who makes the model, where it runs,

2
00:00:04,214 --> 00:00:05,939
and which tool you're using.

3
00:00:06,014 --> 00:00:07,360
Those are three questions.

4
00:00:07,973 --> 00:00:12,073
The maker publishes the model, a
provider or platform offers access,

5
00:00:12,183 --> 00:00:14,383
and the client gives you a workflow.

6
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:17,369
In this lesson, the catalogs are a dated
snapshot.

7
00:00:18,024 --> 00:00:21,045
Your approved picker tells you what is
available for work.

8
00:00:22,518 --> 00:00:27,418
Open the OpenAI model catalog and
compare the entries for GPT six point

9
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:29,318
two Sol and Astra.

10
00:00:29,393 --> 00:00:33,410
In the snapshot, Sol is positioned for
everyday coding and agent work.

11
00:00:34,017 --> 00:00:37,617
Astra is positioned for more demanding
reasoning and coding.

12
00:00:37,717 --> 00:00:40,867
Notice the input and output section on
each card.

13
00:00:40,942 --> 00:00:43,667
If you need transcription or image
generation,

14
00:00:43,742 --> 00:00:46,762
read the relevant entry instead of
inferring it from the name.

15
00:00:48,251 --> 00:00:52,726
In Anthropic’s catalog, find Haiku,
Sonnet, Opus, and Fable.

16
00:00:52,776 --> 00:00:56,573
Their cards describe different speed,
workload, and capability tradeoffs.

17
00:00:57,172 --> 00:01:01,072
The provider table matters when you use
Claude Code remotely.

18
00:01:01,147 --> 00:01:04,972
Two environments can show the same
family name while resolving a shortcut

19
00:01:05,022 --> 00:01:05,530
differently.

20
00:01:06,179 --> 00:01:10,979
Record the exact model ID or selector
label you can verify in the approved

21
00:01:11,029 --> 00:01:11,457
session.

22
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:17,260
In Microsoft Foundry, inspect the
publisher on a model card.

23
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:21,435
The catalog includes models published by
Microsoft as well as models from other

24
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:22,185
providers.

25
00:01:22,787 --> 00:01:26,387
You may see Microsoft’s MAI or Phi
families.

26
00:01:26,462 --> 00:01:30,612
MAI Code two Flash was a limited-access
preview in the snapshot.

27
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:34,312
Check the actual card and deployment
conditions before you put a catalog

28
00:01:34,362 --> 00:01:36,197
model on your team's available list.

29
00:01:37,731 --> 00:01:40,006
Now return to the software you use.

30
00:01:40,081 --> 00:01:44,581
Open Copilot Chat in VS Code and expand
the model selector.

31
00:01:44,656 --> 00:01:47,404
Copy the exact label of one model you
can select.

32
00:01:48,018 --> 00:01:51,093
For a separate check on Claude Code or
OpenCode,

33
00:01:51,143 --> 00:01:54,943
use the approved G C P workspace and
inspect its selector there.

34
00:01:55,598 --> 00:01:57,623
Take the label from each surface.

35
00:01:57,723 --> 00:01:59,266
Those two lists can differ.

36
00:02:00,722 --> 00:02:04,372
Pick a model card and compare its
release status with the workplace

37
00:02:04,422 --> 00:02:05,297
picker.

38
00:02:05,372 --> 00:02:08,056
Preview on the card describes the
provider’s offering.

39
00:02:08,658 --> 00:02:12,658
Your company’s policy, plan, and
configured provider determine whether

40
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:14,158
you can select it.

41
00:02:14,208 --> 00:02:16,558
Put both observations in your note.

42
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:20,233
A colleague can then tell the difference
between a catalog announcement and an

43
00:02:20,283 --> 00:02:21,685
option they can use today.

44
00:02:23,189 --> 00:02:25,664
Make one inventory entry for the class.

45
00:02:25,739 --> 00:02:30,009
Include the maker, the exact version or
label, the client and environment,

46
00:02:30,169 --> 00:02:31,229
and the date you checked.

47
00:02:31,863 --> 00:02:35,563
If the picker changes later, repeat that
short check.

48
00:02:35,613 --> 00:02:39,838
With the available options in hand, the
next lesson will compare models on a bug

49
00:02:39,888 --> 00:02:41,987
whose behavior you can verify yourself.
